---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';
import FaqItem from '../components/FaqItem.astro';
import CtaSection from '../components/CtaSection.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Layout title="FAQs — beCamp">
  <Header />
  <main>
    <PageHero label="Frequently Asked Questions" title="Everything you're wondering before you say yes">
      <Button href="/register" class="mt-12 w-full">Register now [R]</Button>
    </PageHero>

    <section class="px-5 sm:px-20 pt-8 pb-4">
      <div class="divide-y divide-divider border-y border-divider">
        <FaqItem question="Does it cost anything?">
          <p>
            No. beCamp is completely free to attend, including the food, thanks to our
            generous sponsors.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>

        <FaqItem question="What about food and drinks?">
          <p>
            Friday night we'll have pizza, plus a cash bar for drinks. Saturday includes
            breakfast, lunch, and beverages throughout the day.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>

        <FaqItem question="Can I attend just one day?">
          <p>
            You can, but come both days if you're able. You'll get the most out of it
            that way. If you're planning to attend Saturday's sessions, we ask that you
            also come to Friday's Pitch Night, since that's where the schedule gets
            built.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>

        <FaqItem question="Is there a code of conduct?">
          <p>
            Yes. The short version: be kind, be curious, and treat everyone with respect.
            You'll find the full Code of Conduct linked in the footer of every page.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>

        <FaqItem question="I have an accessibility need or a specific request.">
          <p>
            Reach out to us. We want beCamp to work for everyone. Use the "Contact
            organizers" link in the footer and we'll do everything we can to accommodate
            you.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>

        <FaqItem question="Do people bring laptops?">
          <p>
            Many do, and some hands-on sessions make good use of them, but they're not
            required. A notebook and curiosity work just as well.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>

        <FaqItem question="What is beCamp?" open>
          <p>
            Like any good conference: multiple sessions running at once, relevant and
            current topics, a proper lunch break. The differences are that it's local to
            Charlottesville, it's free (including food, thanks to sponsors), and the
            entire agenda gets built by attendees the night before.
          </p>
          <p>Come both days if you can. You'll get the most out of it that way.</p>
          <p>
            beCamp is a genuine barcamp; it's been through years of retrospectives,
            iteration, and a rotating cast of organizers who keep improving the format.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>

        <FaqItem question="Who attends?">
          <p>
            Developers, designers, students, founders, researchers, and the generally
            tech-curious from across the Charlottesville area. You don't need to be an
            expert in anything. If you're interested, you belong.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>

        <FaqItem question="When and where is beCamp 2026?">
          <p>
            November 14–15, 2026. Friday's Pitch Night runs 5–10pm at The Looking Glass
            Art Bar, and Saturday's sessions run 9am–4pm at UVA. Directions to both
            venues are on the home page.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>

        <FaqItem question={'Why does the schedule say "TBD"?'}>
          <p>
            Because it hasn't been written yet, and that's the point. The Saturday
            schedule gets filled in by whoever shows up Friday night, and the empty
            slots are where your topic goes.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>

        <FaqItem question="How are topics decided?">
          <p>
            Anyone can pitch a topic at Friday's Pitch Night, anything they want to
            teach, share, demonstrate, discuss, or understand better. Attendees vote on
            the pitches, and volunteers arrange the winners into Saturday's schedule that
            same evening. You don't need to be an expert to pitch.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>

        <FaqItem question="What happens to topics that don't get picked?">
          <p>
            They don't disappear. Unpicked topics often turn into hallway conversations
            on Saturday, and plenty come back as sharper pitches the following year.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>

        <FaqItem question="How did beCamp get started?">
          <p>
            The first beCamp happened in 2006 in the backyard of local technologist Steve
            Stedman, as an offshoot of the beTech meetup and inspired by BarCamp, the
            open, anyone-welcome answer to Foo Camp. It's been running ever since. The
            full story is on our <a href="/about" class="underline">About page</a>.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>

        <FaqItem question="How can I help?">
          <p>
            Volunteer! There are checkboxes on the registration form for helping out
            Friday or Saturday, and we need and love our volunteers. Organizations can
            also support the event as sponsors, and simply spreading the word helps more
            than you'd think.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>

        <FaqItem question="beCamp vs. beSwarm, what's the difference?">
          <p>
            beSwarm is the spirit of beCamp's Friday night, minus the pitches, run on a
            Saturday. It started in 2010 for people who wanted beCamp more than once a
            year. beCamp is the full two-day unconference; beSwarm is the hangout.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>
      </div>
    </section>

    <CtaSection />
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
